<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Batu Lintang camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Batu Lintang camp was a Japanese internment camp at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a> in Sarawak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, from 1942 to 1945. It held British, Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> prisoners of war together with civilian men, women and children, who suffered starvation and disease. It was liberated by Australian forces in September 1945; the American writer Agnes Newton Keith described it in Three Came Home.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batu-lintang-camp.jpg" alt="Batu Lintang camp" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: not stated · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batu_Lintang_POW_camp,_Sarawak,_Borneo_taken_on_or_after_29_August_1945.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
